  "motif": "M13+M14",
  "tldr": "A sequential experiment that reports an anytime band for an entire quantile treatment-effect curve cannot generally use the pointwise variance-optimal allocation at every quantile. The proposal defines the curve-Neyman regret of an allocation relative to all pointwise oracles and the maximal LIL width of its design-based QTE process. Its first theorem characterizes the sharp minimax Pareto frontier by a one-dimensional convex program with a dual probability measure supported on the active quantiles. Its second theorem derives the fixed-allocation functional LIL from the assignment martingale and finite-population quantile spacing. The open contribution is an observable primal-dual response-adaptive design, coupled to a threshold-indexed self-normalized confidence sequence, that learns each exposed frontier point at the sharp unknown rate while retaining exact time-and-quantile coverage.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Li, Luo, Wang, and Feng derive an efficiency-optimal allocation for one QTE index at a fixed horizon. Noarov, Fogliato, Bertran, and Roth derive anytime Neyman regret for one ATE objective. Howard and Ramdas derive the sharp LIL rate for an i.i.d. quantile process. None characterizes the incompatibility among a continuum of QTE-optimal allocations or links that incompatibility to the width of an anytime design-based band.",
    "niche": "The missing object is a frontier, rather than another allocation for a selected quantile: the first coordinate measures excess design variance relative to every quantile-specific oracle, and the second measures the largest outcome-scale LIL constant over the reported curve.",
    "fill": "The project gives a minimax frontier and its KKT certificate for fixed finite-population potential outcomes, derives the assignment-only functional LIL that turns the variance coordinate into band width, and poses the construction and sharp learning-rate problem for an observable response-adaptive design-confidence-sequence pair."
  },
